// "When you pay for computation" as a narrated film (docs/NARRATED.md "Built contract", shooting script docs/film/models.md).
// One file, 11 scenes, every scene made of the templates in world/scene/film/lib/templates.flecs plus a few hand-written props and beats.
// Everything the narrator says is DRAFT TEXT from the shooting script (Status "draft"): it still needs Andrew's edit before it is approved.
// The voice is a value on the film (Narrator, below): the Qwen3-TTS preset speaker Aiden, no voice of a real person.
// Missing exhibits are honest placeholder cards (tag Placeholder, hatched, labelled "PLACEHOLDER"); nothing fakes an exhibit that does not exist.
// A line's `Text` is what the caption shows; `*word*` marks stress and `[pause]` a breath (both for the writer and the voice; the caption hides them).
// Silence: the line after a hold carries the hold in its `Gap` (the voice file holds the silence); a hold at the end of a scene sits in `Tail`.

models : Film, Narrator {
  Title: {"When you pay for computation"}

  // ---- 1. Cold open: the line the compiler cannot write (0:00 to 0:55) ----
  cold_open : Scene {
    Heading: {"Cold open: the line the compiler cannot write"}
    Covers: {"a type that depends on a value, and the line inference cannot write"}
    Still: {30}
    Tail: {10.8}
    ColdOpen: {
      chapter: "1  Cold open",
      code: "def Tuple : Nat → Type\n  | 0 => Unit\n  | n + 1 => Nat × Tuple n\n\ndef mkTuple : (n : Nat) → Tuple n\n  | 0 => ()\n  | n + 1 => (n, mkTuple n)\n\n#check mkTuple 3   -- mkTuple 3 : Tuple 3\n#eval mkTuple 3    -- (2, 1, 0, ())",
      res: "mkTuple 3 : Tuple 3",
      err: "error: Type mismatch\n  (n, mkTuple n)\nhas type\n  Nat × Unit\nbut is expected to have type\n  Unit",
      ask: "Why can't the compiler write that line?",
      hold: 10,
      l1: "Here's a function. [pause] It builds a tuple. Give it three, and you get two, one, zero, and a unit.",
      l2: "Look at the type. It has a three in it. *The type depends on the value you pass in.*",
      l3: "Now I'll delete one line. Just the signature. [pause] Lean can work out types all day. It should cope. [pause] It doesn't.",
      l4: "Pause the video. Why can't it write that line? Guess.",
      g2: 0.5,
      g3: 0.5,
      g4: 1
    }
  }

  // ---- 2. The question and the misconception (0:55 to 1:25) ----
  question : Scene {
    Heading: {"The question and the misconception"}
    Covers: {"when does a computation happen, and the wall people draw between two kinds of programming"}
    Still: {20}
    MisconceptionTest: {
      chapter: "2  The question",
      l1: "The answer hides in a simpler question. When does a computation happen?",
      l2: "Most of us carry a picture in our heads. There are two kinds of programming. Imperative, where the machine remembers things. Functional, where you write everything down. Two camps with a wall between them.",
      l3: "I'm going to show you there's no wall."
    }
  }

  // ---- 3. A tape that remembers everything (1:25 to 2:15) ----
  tape : Scene {
    Heading: {"A tape that remembers everything"}
    Covers: {"the Turing machine: a head over a tape, and what the program never says"}
    Still: {12}
    DemoPause: {
      chapter: "3  The tape",
      ex: "models/turing",
      preset: 0,
      prompt: "Your turn: press Step and watch the tape",
      note: "binary increment",
      hold: 20,
      l1: "Start with a Turing machine. A head sits over a tape. Each step, it reads a cell, writes a cell, moves, and changes state.",
      l2: "Press step. Watch the tape. This one adds one to a binary number.",
      l3: "Notice what the program never says. It never says what to carry forward. The tape remembers everything, so the program doesn't have to.",
      g3: 20.4
    }
  }

  // ---- 4. A machine with no tape, and an order you can feel (2:15 to 3:25) ----
  lambda : Scene {
    Heading: {"A machine with no tape, and an order you can feel"}
    Covers: {"the lambda calculus, and evaluation order on ignore omega"}
    Still: {30}
    Tail: {15.8}
    DemoPause: {
      chapter: "4  The lambda calculus",
      ex: "models/lambda",
      preset: 0,
      preset2: 1,
      prompt: "Your turn: click the highlighted redex",
      note: "2 = a function that applies its first argument twice",
      hold: 20,
      hold2: 15,
      l1: "Now the other model. The lambda calculus has no tape. Nothing is overwritten. A program is an expression, and running it means plugging arguments into function bodies. If a later step needs something, you have to hand it over.",
      l2: "Here, a number is a function. Two applies its first argument twice. So successor of two is a function applied to a function. Click the highlighted redex and it contracts.",
      l3: "Now the trap. The order of those steps is a choice, and the machine usually hides it. Load \"ignore omega\". Omega loops forever. Ignore throws its argument away.",
      l4: "In normal order, you never touch omega. It finishes. In applicative order, you evaluate the argument first, and it never does. *Same term. Different order.* Try both.",
      g3: 20.4,
      g4: 0.6
    }
  }

  // ---- 5. Two machines, one set of functions (3:25 to 3:55) ----
  two_machines : Scene {
    Heading: {"Two machines, one set of functions"}
    Covers: {"Turing and Church define the same functions; what differs is bookkeeping"}
    Still: {10}
    RevealScene: {
      chapter: "5  One set of functions",
      ex: "models/two-machines",
      big: "Same functions",
      sub: "What differs is bookkeeping",
      card: "1936",
      l1: "So which one is right? [pause] Both. Turing proved in nineteen thirty-six that they define the same functions. Church and Kleene tied lambda terms to the recursive functions the same year.",
      l2: "Neither computes anything the other can't. What differs is bookkeeping."
    }
  }

  // ---- 6. The wall has a door: do-notation (3:55 to 5:00) ----
  do_notation : Scene {
    Heading: {"The wall has a door: do-notation"}
    Covers: {"a do block is the same program as the by-hand version, equal by rfl"}
    Still: {35}
    Equivalence: {
      chapter: "6  Do-notation",
      left: "def tick : StateM Nat Unit := modify (· + 1)\n\ndef threeTicks : StateM Nat Nat := do\n  tick\n  tick\n  tick\n  get\n\n#eval threeTicks.run' 0   -- 3",
      right: "def threeTicksByHand (s : Nat) : Nat × Nat :=\n  let s := s + 1\n  let s := s + 1\n  let s := s + 1\n  (s, s)",
      check: "example : threeTicks = threeTicksByHand := rfl",
      verdict: "accepted by Lean",
      l1: "Here's a counter, written in the imperative style. Tick three times, then read it. Mutable state, right there on the screen.",
      l2: "And here's the same thing with no mutation at all. Each line just makes a new s. Lean's reference gives the rewrite. Every line of a do block becomes a function call that hands the rest of the block to the next step. Haskell's report specifies the same translation.",
      l3: "Now I ask Lean: are these two the same? [pause] It says yes, and the proof is rfl. The two definitions are equal by unfolding alone. *The tape came back as an argument.*"
    }
    // row 4: two portrait cards (placeholders, licences not read) and a heap diagram; the do-notation unroller (exhibit 2) is not built
    ca_1 : CaptionLine {
      Text: {"Moggi gave effects like state a meaning as monads over a pure calculus. Wadler turned that into a way to write programs. And compilers run the conversion the other way: the machine GHC compiles Haskell to keeps unevaluated expressions as closures in a heap."}
      Gap: {0.6}
    }
